[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `fgets` returns `NULL` (immediate EOF) | `""`, `/dev/null`, closed fd 0 | buffer stays `""` → error path |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | newline-terminated | `"5\n"` | trailing `\n` retained in buffer |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | EOF without newline | `"7"` | no `\n` in buffer |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | buffer full at 99 bytes | 98 digits + `'5'`, 200 `'1'`s | 100th byte onward never read |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | newline stops the read | `"5\n9\n"` | second line ignored entirely |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The 99/100-byte cutoff is covered by a sweep (`n` zeros then `"7\n"` for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `n = 95..=105`). This is the one place where an off-by-one is silent on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m happy path: with `99` zeros followed by `9`, the significant digit is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m truncated away and the value is `0`, not `9`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `parse_val()` — `strtol` + three guards
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Guard | Reached by | Result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `endp != str` fails (no conversion) | `"abc"`, `"   \n"`, `"-"`, `"+"`, `"- 5"`, `"--5"`, `".5"`, `"\x00 5"`, `"\xff5"` | `An error occurred` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `errno == 0` fails (`ERANGE`) | `"9223372036854775808"`, `"-9223372036854775809"`, 20+ nines, 99 nines | `An error occurred` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `tmp >= INT_MIN` fails | `"-2147483649"`, `"-4294967296"`, `"-9223372036854775808"` | `An error occurred` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `tmp <= INT_MAX` fails | `"2147483648"`, `"4294967296"`, `"9223372036854775807"` | `An error occurred` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | all guards pass | `"0"`, `"5"`, `"-3"`, `"+7"`, `"2147483647"`, `"-2147483648"` | `run(x); run(x);` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtol` semantics that had to be reproduced exactly, each with a test:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - leading whitespace is skipped, using C `isspace` in the "C" locale — space,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `\t`, `\n`, `\v` (`0x0b`), `\f` (`0x0c`), `\r`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - base 10, so `"0x1f"` parses as `0` with `endp` after the `0`; because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `endp != str`, this is a **success** returning `0`, not an error.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - a partial parse succeeds: `"12abc"`, `"2.5"`, `"1e5"`, `"1_000"`, `"12,34"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   all yield the leading integer.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `strtol` operates on the C string, so an embedded NUL terminates the scan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`"12\0" + "34"` → `12`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - on overflow the return value saturates to `LONG_MIN`/`LONG_MAX` *and* sets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ERANGE`. Both are observable, but here the `ERANGE` guard fires first, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the saturated value never reaches the int-range guards.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - glibc's `strtol` does **not** set `errno` when no conversion is performed, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the `endp != str` guard is what rejects `"abc"`. This was confirmed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   empirically rather than assumed, since POSIX permits `EINVAL` here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `run()` and the mutable global `the_house`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `the_house` is a file-scope global, so the second `run(x)` continues from the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m state the first one left behind. This is the most load-bearing detail in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program: floors go `2 → 3 → 4`, bathrooms `2.5 → 3.5 → 4.5`, and bedrooms
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `5 → 5 + x → 5 + 2x`. `golden_happy_path_exact_bytes` pins all eight output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m lines as literal bytes so this cannot regress unnoticed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `add_bedrooms` performs `house->bedrooms += extra_bedrooms` twice on an `int`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m For large `x` this overflows — formally UB in C, wrapping in practice on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build targets used here. The Rust uses `wrapping_add`, which matches; tests
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cover `INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN`, `715827882`, `-715827883` and `1073741824`, chosen
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so the overflow happens on the first or the second `run` respectively. Note
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that the release profile sets `panic = "abort"`, so a plain `+=` would abort
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in debug builds instead of wrapping; `wrapping_add` is required, not stylistic.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%.1f", ...)` only ever formats `2.5`, `3.5` and `4.5`, all exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m representable in binary floating point, so no rounding-mode difference between
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C's `%.1f` and Rust's `{:.1}` can arise here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Non-stdin behaviour checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `argv` is ignored by both (`main()` takes no parameters).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdin closed, stdin from `/dev/null`, and stdin redirected from a directory
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   all take the error path in both, exiting `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdout redirected to `/dev/full`, and stdout closed early by a downstream
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reader, produce the same exit status from both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known residual difference (not reachable through normal invocation)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` at startup, whereas the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inherits the default disposition. If stdout were a pipe whose reader closed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before the program's ~450 bytes of output were flushed, the C could die from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIGPIPE` (status 141) while the Rust would ignore the failed write and exit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0`. The direct test of this scenario had both exit `0`, because the output is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m small enough to be delivered before the reader closes. Fixing it would require
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m resetting the signal disposition through `libc`, which the crate does not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m depend on. It is recorded here as the one observable divergence that a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sufficiently adversarial harness could provoke; no input fed through stdin can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m trigger it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Negative control
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m To confirm the suite is not vacuous, three bugs were injected into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/src/main.rs` one at a time and the suite was re-run:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Injected bug | Caught by |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | int-range guard widened to `INT_MAX + 1` | `int_range_boundaries` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `fgets` capacity off by one (100 payload bytes instead of 99) | `fgets_truncates_at_99_bytes` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `wrapping_add` → `saturating_add` in `add_bedrooms` | `int_range_boundaries`, `long_range_errors_set_errno`, `pseudorandom_inputs` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each injected bug produced a failure; the source was restored and verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical afterwards, and the suite passes again.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Reproducing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd ../../translation && cargo test --release
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The test harness builds the C reference itself if `c_src/build/driver` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m absent. All 17 tests pass; none is `#[ignore]`d, skipped or disabled.
